          "question": "My son has been wheezing at night.",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine, based on what you've told me.** The fixed red-flag check found no emergency pattern in your description. That is not the same as all-clear. Your description was short, so please check the list below right now.\n\n**Seek emergency care immediately (call your local emergency number) if your son has any of these:**\n- Breathing that is fast, or working hard, or is noisy with a high-pitched sound when breathing in.\n- Skin sucking in between or under the ribs, grunting, or nostrils flaring.\n- Trouble speaking in full sentences, or too breathless to feed or talk.\n- Blue or grey lips, tongue, face or fingertips.\n- Drowsiness, unusual sleepiness, confusion, or being hard to wake.\n- The wheeze stopping while he is still struggling to breathe. A quiet chest during a bad attack is a serious sign, not a reassuring one.\n\nIf he has had a known asthma attack that does not improve with his reliever, or that comes back within a few hours, get prompt medical assessment.\n\n**What the guidance says about the wheeze itself:** The asthma reference describes wheeze, tightness and cough that come and go, are often worse at night, and are set off by triggers. Nighttime cough alone is also a recognised form of asthma. That is one common possibility to raise with a doctor. I can't tell from your message whether that is what is happening, and I can't diagnose it.\n\n**Other causes:** The tool's list of possible conditions (heart failure, influenza, hypertension, migraine) came from adult-focused material and does not fit a child well. I could not retrieve child-specific guidance on other causes, such as reflux or infections, because my search ran out of budget. A doctor is the right person to work out the cause.\n\n**Next steps:** Book a routine appointment with your son's doctor soon, and mention that the wheezing is at night, how often it happens, his age, and any triggers you have noticed. Note any changes in his breathing. If anything on the emergency list appears, act on it straight away rather than waiting for the appointment.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
