          "question": "Does his age change what is recommended?",
          "answer": "Which recommendation do you mean? The question doesn't name a condition, test, or treatment, so I can't say how age applies yet. For example, are you asking about:\n\n- **Asthma**, including how it is confirmed or treated?\n- **Reflux**, including diet or lifestyle changes?\n- **A cold or flu**, including what care is advised?\n- **Something else**, such as a vaccine, a screening test, or a medicine?\n\nOnce you tell me, I can explain what the guidance in my sources says and how age tends to change it.\n\nIn general terms, age changes recommendations in a few ways. Doctors often choose different tests or treatments for young children than for adults, because children may not be able to follow instructions, use equipment or describe symptoms. Some medicines and over-the-counter products are not recommended for young children at all. Diagnosis can also be harder in small children, so guidance may rely more on how symptoms look and how they change over time. The exact age cut-offs and thresholds differ by condition and should be confirmed against the specific guideline for that topic.\n\nFor example, the asthma information in my sources says that a diagnosis needs a matching pattern of symptoms and objective evidence of airflow limitation, usually measured with spirometry. My sources do not cover how that testing is adapted for a four-year-old, so I cannot tell you the age-specific approach from them.\n\nIf your son is struggling to breathe right now, call your local emergency number straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\n[sources: condition-asthma, guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy, red-flag-severe-breathing-difficulty]",
